# Implement joined strokes diacritics and correlated variation

## Scope and existing evidence

Compose accents and joined cursive forms from exact base geometry. Let the
user edit stroke order and direction, reverse a stroke deliberately, and
compare ideal versus varied ink with visible controls for thickness, height,
regularity, spacing and slant.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P4-005 — archived handwriting design
- ATR-P4-006 — archived handwriting design
- ATR-P4-007 — archived handwriting design

## Ordered-line editing (partial) — 2026-11-03

The browser editor can select an existing segment, reverse its endpoint
direction, and move it before or after the adjacent segment belonging to
the same exact Unicode grapheme, preserving unrelated entries and allowing
explicit TSV roundtrip. Focused tests cover interleaved Unicode glyphs,
first/last boundary disablement, unchanged neighbors, and session reset.
This records **segment order**, not continuous curve grouping, pen lifts,
joined cursive, accent composition or seeded correlated ink variation.
Those acceptance criteria remain open.

## Explicit diacritic addition from authored bases (partial) — 2026-11-03

The glyph editor can append an ordered accent, tilde or diaeresis in the
exact selected Unicode target from existing user-authored base strokes.
Composition keeps the base untouched, copies its stroke sequence to the
target and refuses incompatible targets or clipped accent segments. This
provides an executable, opt-in diacritic composition example; it does not
preserve a live stroke/mark association through later edits. Correlated
seeded variation, joined cursive, multi-stroke pen lifts and continuous
curve primitives are still open.

## Dotless acute i composition (partial) — 2026-11-03

The explicit compose-accent action now handles NFC `í` and NFD `i` plus
combining acute from the already-authored two-stroke dotted lowercase `i`.
It copies the stem unchanged and attaches one bounded acute, omitting only
an independently identifiable short upper dot from the new target. The
original dotted `i` remains unchanged. Unrecognized multi-stroke or
ambiguous dot constructions refuse instead of deleting user ink.

Three focused browser regressions verify the two exact Unicode targets,
source preservation, dotless stroke order, UTF-8 native TSV roundtrip,
refusal of missing/overlapping dots and the opt-in browser composition
workflow. The native Rust glyph CLI read the canonical TSV byte-for-byte,
audited `íí` as **two independent exact Unicode graphemes with two strokes
each and zero missing**, then exported real one-page PDF and transparent
72-DPI RGBA PNG. This is a one-time explicit stroke copy, not
an attached analytic accent or automatic font substitution; cursive and
seeded correlated handwriting remain open.

## Fill-missing Spanish lowercase accents (partial) — 2026-11-03

After the caller explicitly authors the lowercase bases, the localhost
editor can fill only missing NFC `á é í ó ú ñ ü` with one admitted-session
click. Existing custom accented ink is never replaced. The complete batch
is checked before changing the in-memory draft, and still requires an
explicit TSV download. A missing source, ambiguous dotted `i`, clipped
accent or out-of-cap library refuses all pending changes. NFD targets
remain independent, not normalized into the generated NFC keys.

Two browser regressions cover the session gate, seven ordered target
strokes from existing bases, idempotence, custom NFC and NFD preservation,
and atomic refusal. The combined lowercase-plus-accent draft has **986
sampled native strokes**; Rust read it byte-for-byte, audited all seven
NFC accents with **zero missing shapes**, and rendered `niño` as a real
one-page PDF and transparent 72-DPI RGBA PNG. This is controlled manual
starter composition, not automatic full bilingual coverage or joined
cursive ink. Seeded correlated variation remains open.

## Reusable curved n/u sources (partial) — 2026-11-03

Two explicitly selected rounded-letter starters now supply mathematical
quadratic guides and ordered n/u line samples. The existing exact-glyph
accent action can append tilde/diaeresis to these samples. Neither
composition nor export retains live joins or a parametric curve primitive;
seeded correlated handwriting variation remains unfinished.

## Seeded ideal-versus-varied browser ink comparison (partial) — 2026-11-03

The authenticated glyph editor now provides a six-copy SVG comparison of
one exact user-authored Unicode grapheme, including authored accents. The
first row has three ideal repetitions; the second independently varies each
repetition using a caller-specified uint32 seed. Six bounded spread controls
apply glyph-correlated width, height, thickness and slant, plus per-occurrence
horizontal spacing and endpoint regularity. Shared stroke endpoints derive
jitter from their physical coordinates and seed, so they remain connected
and unaffected by stroke reordering. Invalid amplitudes, seed, zero-length
strokes, clipped pen width/geometry and more than 128 selected strokes
refuse the varied preview instead of changing any authored ink.

Three additional focused regressions cover exact NFC `á` geometry, pen-order
invariance, replay and distinct occurrence seeds, a real browser two-row
accented `ó` preview, spacing-only changes and invalid input refusal.
The TSV downloaded before and after changing the preview seed is byte-for-byte
identical; session end clears the preview. All **75 focused glyph tests**
and the TypeScript check/build pass. An existing real-Firefox/Rust localhost
journey now verifies that authored `ó` produces a 294-line six-copy preview,
seed changes alter only the lower row, and invalid width spread refuses
without deleting the 49 original glyph strokes. This is not a native page
or PDF/PNG handwriting variation renderer: the seeded samples are
preview-only until individually exported (see below). There is no joined
cursive or persisted correlation across lines or words. The task remains
open for the full finite two-line native output acceptance.

## Explicit derived varied glyph TSV (partial) — 2026-11-03

The admitted browser editor can now deliberately download any of the three
seeded lower-row occurrences for the selected exact Unicode grapheme as a
**separate** v1 glyph TSV. The full authored library is copied and only
that glyph's matched strokes are substituted; the unsaved canonical draft,
other glyph keys, and ordinary **Download stroke TSV** bytes stay intact.
One export rechecks the uint32 seed, all six variation bounds, the chosen
sample (1–3), the 128-stroke preview limit, physical pen margins, and the
4096-stroke/512-KiB complete TSV caps. Disabled controls do not grant file
writes; invalid input refuses without a downloaded file.

A new browser regression verifies sample 2 of authored `ó` equals the
calculated 49 varied lines, `∞` is unchanged, sample 3 differs, invalid
sample/spread refuses, and the canonical TSV remains byte-identical after
variant downloads. **76 focused glyph tests** and TypeScript check/build
pass. The independent Rust CLI accepted a 49-stroke native `ó` variant with
**zero missing exact glyphs**, read it back byte-for-byte and emitted a real
one-page PDF and transparent 596×842 RGBA PNG at 72 DPI containing `óó`.

The real Firefox/Rust localhost acceptance journey now independently
captures the **actual** `my-varied-glyph-strokes.tsv` download Blob after
clicking the admitted sample-2 button (not a test-only surrogate encoder).
Its UTF-8 bytes are written only to a disposable test directory, reopened
by the separate Rust CLI with exact byte equality, audited as `ó` with
49 authored strokes and zero missing glyphs, and rendered to both actual
PDF and PNG. The test restores temporary browser API interception and
removes its transient session/files afterward.

This derived TSV makes the native PDF draw the **same chosen variant**
for both `ó` repetitions. Native per-occurrence sampling is available
through the separate CLI option below, not by this sampled TSV alone;
neither option persists variation settings across sessions.

## Native per-occurrence glyph variation on PDF/PNG (partial) — 2026-11-03

The Rust `atrament-profiles notebook` CLI now accepts an **explicit**
`--glyph-variation SEED WIDTH HEIGHT THICKNESS SLANT SPACING REGULARITY`
option alongside a user-authored TSV or ink segment library. It applies
browser-compatible seeded width/height/thickness, slant, spacing and
shared-endpoint regularity at **each visible grapheme occurrence** rather
than replacing its canonical glyph source. Samples continue in text-run
order across physical pages, with the same exact NFC/NFD glyph identities;
flow uses the original nominal advances and physical overshoot refuses.
The 128-stroke-per-glyph browser sample bound and pen/cell clearance still
apply, before native output is committed. The existing `--handwriting-profile`
mean transforms apply only after the optional local perturbation.

One regression checks all nine native line segments and tracking offsets
for three authored accented `á` copies against independently sampled
browser exact-coordinate fixtures, including shared vertices and repeat
identity. A second renders four independent `ó` instances on two genuine
physical PDF pages and transparent PNG page 2, checking stable replay and
separate shapes across pages; a third rejects invalid seeds/spreads,
duplicate settings and shape-free requests without replacing an existing
file. A separate regression verifies decomposed Unicode `n` plus tilde
using explicit authored fallback ink and selected per-grapheme means.
Native Rust CLI tests cover these cases, PDF/PNG and error refusals;
strict production-binary Clippy passes. No derived sample
is written back into the authored library.

This is **not** full P1-014 acceptance: per-line/word correlation, joined
cursive and stroke grouping remain unavailable; the CLI variation settings,
text and page content are not persisted in a saved notebook. A browser
control still does not directly command native per-occurrence rendering.

## Native exact-Unicode stroke-order inspection (partial) — 2026-11-03

The Rust glyph library CLI now supports stdout-only
`glyphs --profile glyphs.tsv --inspect-glyph GRAPHEME` to print the exact
input-sequence segment index, start/end micrometre positions, pen width and
RGB for a single authored Unicode grapheme. Interleaved glyph records are
filtered without reordering; precomposed and combining-accent sequences
remain separate identities. The report does not infer pen lifts, cursive
joins or continuous geometric paths and cannot write or mutate a file.

Two Rust regressions verify an interleaved NFC/NFD library roundtrip,
full ordered/directional report parity for inline and saved TSV, actual
native PDF paint order, and unchanged input/target files when inspection
receives invalid, duplicate, missing, conflicting or output-selecting
arguments. This makes the existing browser reordering auditable by the
native pipeline, not a persisted stroke-group or cursive model. A separate
browser-generated 119-glyph TSV -> native Rust integration now checks every
ordered segment and pen direction for `ó`, decomposed `o` plus acute, `ñ`,
and decomposed `n` plus tilde, and verifies unchanged saved TSV bytes before
rendering the same full PDF/PNG pages. The original full handwriting
acceptance remains open.

## Acceptance decision

Open. The finite implementation described above is not established by
the original theoretical design acceptance. This follow-up must be
evaluated separately against its practical or theoretical scope.
